{"gypsy":{"id":"44b4371f-1c25-4af2-bf4b-e38c89c8448f","word":"gypsy","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"any itinerant person, or any person suspected of making a living from dishonest practices or theft","tags":["masculine"],"word":"cigano"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"any itinerant person, or any person suspected of making a living from dishonest practices or theft","tags":["feminine"],"word":"cigana"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"It was at this town of Badajoz, the capital of Estremadura, that I first fell in with those singular people, the Zincali, Gitanos, or Spanish gypsies.","ref":"1843, George Borrow, The Bible in Spain, London: Cassell and Company, Ltd.:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[142,149]],"id":"8d386bc8-8584-4634-a22c-2d9d06194275"},{"text":"He championed the music of the gypsies as he did the Muslim and Jewish roots of Spanish culture. […] All these and more were fused in the forge of the gypsy experience into a singular art form, unlike any of its sources, evoking its own worldview, its own existential stance.","ref":"2010 February 5, Mike Marqusee, “Flamenco's humane roots”, in The Guardian:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[31,38],[151,156]],"id":"68ae74d1-cb64-48ca-a159-b75406d19e84"}],"categories":["English offensive terms","English terms with quotations"],"links":[["Gypsy","Gypsy#English"]],"raw_glosses":["(sometimes offensive) Alternative form of Gypsy (“member of the Romani people”)."],"glosses":["Alternative form of Gypsy (“member of the Romani people”)."],"tags":["alt-of","alternative","offensive","sometimes"],"alt_of":[{"word":"Gypsy","extra":"member of the Romani people"}],"id":"3ec46819-70bf-45ab-bba1-2bbe4ba54971"},{"examples":[{"text":"Like a right gypsy, hath, at fast and loose, Beguiled me to the very heart of loss.","ref":"c. 1606–1607 (date written), William Shakespeare, “The Tragedie of Anthonie and Cleopatra”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act II, scene vii]:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[13,18]],"id":"63e44662-5a12-4130-8bca-1e98faffd08d"}],"categories":["English colloquialisms","English terms with quotations"],"links":[["itinerant","itinerant"],["Romani","Romani"],["tinker","tinker"],["traveller","traveller"],["carny","carny"]],"raw_glosses":["(colloquial) An itinerant person or any person, not necessarily Romani; a tinker, a traveller or a carny."],"glosses":["An itinerant person or any person, not necessarily Romani; a tinker, a traveller or a carny."],"tags":["colloquial"],"id":"045c1c22-809c-495c-9e1e-7dd7bee1def2"},{"categories":["English offensive terms"],"links":[["contra dancing","contra dancing"],["swing","swing"],["whole gyp","whole gyp#English"],["half gyp","half gyp#English"],["gypsy meltdown","gypsy meltdown#English"]],"raw_glosses":["(sometimes offensive) A move in contra dancing in which two dancers walk in a circle around each other while maintaining eye contact (but not touching as in a swing). (Compare whole gyp, half gyp, and gypsy meltdown, in which this step precedes a swing.)"],"glosses":["A move in contra dancing in which two dancers walk in a circle around each other while maintaining eye contact (but not touching as in a swing). (Compare whole gyp, half gyp, and gypsy meltdown, in which this step precedes a swing.)"],"tags":["offensive","sometimes"],"id":"3c0db1eb-3763-47fc-bfbc-5753ed0e29c2"},{"topics":["entertainment","lifestyle","theater"],"categories":["en:Theater"],"links":[["theater","theater"],["Broadway","Broadway"],["musical","musical"],["chorus line","chorus line"]],"raw_glosses":["(theater) A member of a Broadway musical chorus line."],"glosses":["A member of a Broadway musical chorus line."],"id":"344e563f-16f0-405f-8fba-1ecc92958a96"},{"categories":["English dated terms"],"links":[["dark","dark"],["complexion",